<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Colophon</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Colophon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ionian city famed for its cavalry and wealth, home of the poets Mimnermus and Xenophanes and keeper of the oracle of Apollo at Claros.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Colophon lay inland north-west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus</a> and was founded by Ionian settlers c. 1000 BCE. It was one of the twelve cities of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Ionian League</a> and in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE grew rich and powerful, its exiles seizing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolians">Aeolians</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Colophon had a strong landed aristocracy whose cavalry was proverbial. The elegiac poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimnermus">Mimnermus</a> and the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophanes">Xenophanes</a> were Colophonians. The oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claros">Claros</a> lay in its territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia">Gyges</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia</a> captured its lower town in the 7th century BCE, and it then passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> forced many Colophonians to move to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus</a> c. 294 BCE. The city recovered, partly through its port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notion-ancient-city">Notion</a>, called New Colophon, and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 133 BCE, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claros">Claros</a> remained a major oracle into Roman times.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:39.80%;width:17.25%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Colophon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; oracle of Apollo at Claros</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kolophon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=colophon" aria-label="Colophon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5356 808 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colophon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5676 1064L5697 1052M5676 1064L6140 1251M5676 1064L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 1052h0M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5676 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5676 1064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Colophon. <a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=colophon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

